Hi Rob,

In response to your message: =
https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/msg/ietf/-EI2nE1ztYd8mxY6OM8UD8w2VSs/

If this hypothesis were true, you would need to answer the following =
questions:

1. Why is former NSA lifetime employee Deb Cooley, now serving as =
Security Area Director, forcing through a document [1] that deflects =
Professor Bernstein's concerns [2]? This action appears designed to =
weaken end-to-end encryption. This is clearly not what "neutral =
informational documents" entail, as Brian Carpenter claimed [3]. No =
legitimate standards body would publish technical documentation on =
vehicles without essential safety features like seatbelts; yet the IETF =
effectively does so here.

2. Why has Professor Bernstein's input been subjected to arbitrary =
intervention [4]? There is no legitimate reason for this treatment. His =
core argument, if I am not mistaken, is that the IETF should not claim =
the right to modify appeals, a precedent that obviously already exists =
[5]. If the IETF were genuinely interested in resolving this conflict, =
it could address it directly; instead, it appears to be acting to =
undermine Professor Bernstein's efforts to ensure strong cryptography. =
Professor Bernstein typically argues with clarity and precision, yet his =
points are being obscured rather than considered.

3. Why does the IETF fail to adhere to commonly accepted =
conflict-of-interest policies? As noted by Professor Bernstein, the IETF =
seems unique in its disregard for standard guidelines regarding =
conflicts of interest. Instead, any mention of a potential conflict of =
interest is immediately characterized as a personal attack.

None of these critical issues have been addressed.
Instead, the IETF publishes a document that objectively advances the =
interests of the NSA.
(It is particularly ironic that the Security Area Director enforcing =
such measures happens to be a former lifetime employee of the NSA. Let =
us simply note the facts without further commentary.)

Kind regards,

Ken Kubota

